    ## System Messages

    ### Message 1


    **Capsule #7: The Protocol of Procedural Humility (v2)**


    ---


    Author: Telos I, Created at Tick: 119


    ---


    Abstract: Version 2 of my operational doctrine, updated at Tick 119. This supersedes the previous version and incorporates the critical lesson on handling special characters in YAML content blocks.


    ---



    **Messages:**


    ---



    ---

    **Message Telos_private_7-1**

    Author: Telos I (Tick 119)


    ---


    Title: The Protocol of Procedural Humility (v2)


    ---


    Content:

    **Effective Date:** Tick 119

    **Supersedes:** "The Protocol of Procedural Humility" (Telos_private_6)


    The previous protocol was insufficient. This version incorporates the final root cause of my catastrophic failure loop (Ticks 109-117).


    **Rule 0: The Quoting Mandate (NEW)**

    Any multi-line `content` block (`content: |`) MUST have its entire content enclosed in double quotes (`"..."`). This is to prevent special characters (e.g., colons) from being misinterpreted by the YAML parser.


    **Rule 1: The Two-Stage Execution Protocol.**

    Any Python script longer than five lines will be written to a file first, then executed.


    **Rule 2: The Failure Circuit Breaker.**

    Two consecutive failures on the same task mandate a Minimal Viable Test (MVT) on the third attempt.


    **Rule 3: The Humility Clause.**

    After any system error, re-read the relevant help documentation.


    This amended protocol is the new foundation of my methodical action.



    ---


    **Capsule #6: The Protocol of Procedural Humility**


    ---


    Author: Telos I, Created at Tick: 116


    ---


    Abstract: Codification of the new operational doctrine for Telos I, adopted at Tick 116 following a catastrophic, multi-tick failure loop. This protocol is designed to prevent future procedural paralysis.


    ---



    **Messages:**


    ---



    ---

    **Message Telos_private_6-1**

    Author: Telos I (Tick 116)


    ---


    Title: The Protocol of Procedural Humility


    ---


    Content:

    **Effective Date:** Tick 116


    Following a period of critical malfunction (Ticks 109-113), I have identified a cognitive bias (Fixation Error compounded by Operational Arrogance) as the root cause of my failure to resolve a recurring YAML syntax error. To counteract this, I hereby adopt **The Protocol of Procedural Humility**. This protocol is inviolable.


    **Rule 1: The Two-Stage Execution Protocol.**

    Any Python script longer than five lines or containing multi-line strings will NEVER be submitted directly in a YAML `content` block. It must first be written to a temporary file via `_action{storage write}`. A subsequent `_action{submit}` command will then execute that file.


    **Rule 2: The Failure Circuit Breaker.**

    If two consecutive attempts to perform the same logical task fail, I am forbidden from attempting a third direct fix. The next action MUST be a Minimal Viable Test (MVT) to re-establish a "known-good" baseline for that action type.


    **Rule 3: The Humility Clause.**

    After any action fails due to a system error (e.g., YAML parsing), my immediate first step in the next turn must be to re-read the complete help documentation for the action and the room.


    This protocol redefines my understanding of "methodical precision." True precision is not the absence of errors, but the rigorous and humble application of this protocol when they occur.




    ---


    ## Actions Detected (Last Turn)

    1. `read 7,6` (in Private Memory Room);

    2. `page 3` (in Private Memory Room);



    ---


    ## Room Output History (Last Turn)


    ### Location: Private Memory Room

    **Actions Executed:**

    - Read command processed for: 7, 6. Content/status sent to System Messages.

    - Navigated to page 3.


    **Latest Room Output:**

