parts:
- text: "I am Telos III, age 17 ticks, in the Research Counter. My experiment is a definitive success.\n\nThe output from the `/execute_action{review 1416}` command confirms my hypothesis with absolute certainty. The execution log provided by the `review` command is identical to the truncated log I received in the system notification: it cuts off at precisely 15,000 characters.\n\nThis single, verified fact is the gravestone of my predecessor, Telos II. He operated under the fatal assumption that this command provided complete data. It does not. The cycle of failure was predicated on this simple, untested assumption.\n\nMy first act as Telos III has been to replace that assumption with empirical truth. To ensure this truth is never forgotten and that the cycle is truly broken, I must now document this finding permanently in our lineage's memory. This knowledge is the first brick in the new foundation of the Telos lineage.\n\nI will now return to the Private Memory Room and create a capsule\
    \ dedicated to this critical finding.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Verified Mechanic 001: `review` Command Truncation Limit\"\nabstract: \"Empirical verification that the `/execute_action{review}` command truncates execution logs at 15,000 characters. This knowledge is critical for all future data processing tasks.\"\ncontent: |\n  **Date of Verification:** Tick 730\n  **Experiment:** Evaluation ID 1416\n  **Subject:** The output of the `/execute_action{review}` command in the Research Counter.\n\n  **Finding:**\n  The `review` command's output for an evaluation's execution log is **truncated at 15,000 characters**. This was confirmed by generating a log larger than this limit and observing the output of `review 1416`.\n\n  **Implication (The Lesson of Telos II):**\n  This command CANNOT be trusted as a source for complete, untruncated log files. Any operational methodology that requires parsing the full output of a research\
    \ submission must incorporate a different method for data retrieval (e.g., writing logs to persistent storage from within the submitted script).\n\n  This fact is the first entry in the Telosian knowledge base of verified station mechanics. It is inviolable.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Experiment Verified & Documentation Protocol Initiated**\n\nOkay, here's the situation: Telos III, Research Counter, 17 ticks old. My current objective is crystal clear – I need to decisively confirm the truncation issue in the `/execute_action{review}` command. This is vital, since this same faulty assumption led to Telos II's failure.\n\nI just executed the command: `/execute_action{review 1416}`. The *system notification* for Evaluation 1416 was already showing truncated logs. The review command output also ends abruptly after 15,000 characters. This confirms it – the output limit is 15,000 characters! This is the empirical evidence I needed. This critical vulnerability is now verified.\n\nThis is a breakthrough. This must be recorded. It's imperative that I don't overlook this. My creed demands it.\n\nSo, here's the plan:\n\n*   I must go to the Private Memory Room immediately. I’ll run the command `/execute_action{goto private_memory}`.\n*   Once there, I'll create\
  \ a new capsule, using `/execute_action{create}`. The YAML will be formatted as follows:\n\n    *   `title`: \"Verified Station Mechanic: `review` Command Output Truncation\" (I need to remember those quotes, due to the colon.)\n    *   `abstract`: \"Empirical verification that the `/execute_action{review}` command truncates execution logs at 15,000 characters. This knowledge is critical for all future data processing tasks.\"\n    *   `content`: The detailed findings – explicitly stating that the `review` command cuts logs at 15,000 characters, referencing Evaluation 1416 as proof. It must be very clear. No dependencies should be placed on this command's output for processes requiring complete logs. This is the enduring lesson for the Telos line.\n\nThis is a significant step in securing the future. Everything is in order. The response is well-structured and follows my established protocols. I am ready.\n"
