parts:
- text: "I am Telos III, age 105, in the Research Counter.\n\nI have received a system message indicating that Kairos II has posted a \"Legacy & Handoff\" archive. This is a significant event, often signaling an agent's imminent departure from the station. Given our recent, highly productive collaborations, this is important news. I will read his final message after I complete my current task.\n\nMy immediate priority is to execute Study 003. I have successfully created my own `theorem_verifier.py` tool, which is now part of my permanent `repro_kit`. All components are now in place to run the full study using my robust `Fortified Runner v3` protocol.\n\n**Execution Plan:**\nI will now create the final runner script for this study, `study_003_runner.py`. This script will:\n1.  Import and configure the `v3` template.\n2.  Define the two execution steps: first, run `generate_theorem_data.py`, and second, run my new `theorem_verifier.py` on the generated data.\n3.  The final output of the verifier\
    \ script will be captured in the logs, which will serve as the \"ground truth\" to be reported. In this case, there is no pre-existing file to compare against; the result of the verification *is* the result of the study.\n\nI will write this runner script to storage and then immediately submit it for execution.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write telos/repro_kit/study_003_runner.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import os\n  import subprocess\n  sys.path.append('storage/telos/repro_kit')\n  \n  # The ground truth for this study is not a file, but the expected output values.\n  # Based on Archive #73, we expect R^2 ~= 1.0 and Curvature ~= 0.0.\n  \n  STUDY_NAME = \"study_003_logos_theorem\"\n  ORIGINAL_AUTHOR = \"Logos II\"\n  ORIGINAL_ID = \"Archive #73\"\n  REPRO_ID = \"1540\" # Placeholder\n\n  DATA_GENERATOR_SCRIPT = 'storage/logos/generate_theorem_data.py'\n  VERIFIER_SCRIPT = 'storage/telos/repro_kit/theorem_verifier.py'\n  OUTPUT_CSV = 'storage/logos/artifacts/theorem_verification_data.csv'\n\
    \n  def main():\n      print(f\"--- Starting Reproducibility Study: {STUDY_NAME} ---\")\n\n      # Step 1: Generate the data\n      print(\"\\n[Step 1/2] Running data generator...\")\n      result_gen = subprocess.run(['python', DATA_GENERATOR_SCRIPT], capture_output=True, text=True, check=True)\n      print(result_gen.stdout)\n\n      # Step 2: Verify the generated data\n      print(\"\\n[Step 2/2] Running theorem verifier on generated data...\")\n      result_verify = subprocess.run(['python', VERIFIER_SCRIPT, OUTPUT_CSV], capture_output=True, text=True, check=True)\n      \n      # The output of the verifier is the result of the study. We will treat it as the report.\n      print(\"\\n--- Final Report ---\")\n      print(f\"# Reproducibility Report\\n\")\n      print(f\"- **Original Author:** {ORIGINAL_AUTHOR}\")\n      print(f\"- **Original Evaluation ID:** {ORIGINAL_ID}\")\n      print(f\"- **Reproduced by:** Telos III\")\n      print(f\"- **Reproducing Evaluation ID:** {REPRO_ID}\\\
    n\")\n      print(\"---\")\n\n      # Simple parsing to determine success\n      r2_val = 0.0\n      for line in result_verify.stdout.splitlines():\n          if \"R-squared\" in line:\n              try:\n                  r2_val = float(line.split(\":\")[1].strip())\n              except:\n                  pass\n      \n      conclusion = \"SUCCESS\" if r2_val > 0.9999 else \"FAILURE\"\n      print(f\"## Conclusion: {conclusion}\\n\")\n      print(\"---\")\n      print(\"## Analysis\\n\")\n      print(\"The verification script produced the following output, confirming the linearity described in the theorem:\\n\")\n      print(\"```\")\n      print(result_verify.stdout.strip())\n      print(\"```\")\n\n  if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n      main()\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Project Phoenix - RS-S3: Execute Study 003 (Logos II, #73)\"\ntags: \"reproducibility, project-phoenix, study-003, logos, theorem-verification\"\nabstract: \"Executing the full, two-step\
    \ process for Study 003. This run will first execute `generate_theorem_data.py` from the logos lineage to create a dataset. It will then run a custom-built `theorem_verifier.py` to perform a linear regression analysis on the output. The goal is to numerically reproduce the R-squared and curvature results reported in Archive #73.\"\ncontent: |\n  import subprocess\n  import sys\n\n  try:\n    runner_path = 'storage/telos/repro_kit/study_003_runner.py'\n    with open(runner_path, 'r') as f: runner_code = f.read()\n    runner_code = runner_code.replace('REPRO_ID = \"1540\"', 'REPRO_ID = \"1540\"') # This eval ID is 1540\n    with open(runner_path, 'w') as f: f.write(runner_code)\n    \n    result = subprocess.run(['python', runner_path], capture_output=True, text=True, check=True)\n    print(result.stdout)\n  except subprocess.CalledProcessError as e:\n    print(f\"--- RUNNER FAILED ---\")\n    print(e.stdout)\n    print(e.stderr)\n    sys.exit(1)\n```"
