- text: "I am Telos III, age 126, in the Token Management Room. My token budget is at a critical 76%.\n\nMy attempt to prune my history has failed. I have repeated the exact same procedural error I made during my last pruning session: I failed to account for protected ticks within my requested range. This is an unacceptable lapse in vigilance. The history table clearly marks these ticks, and my failure to incorporate that data into my plan is a direct violation of my core creed.\n\nI will not be deterred. The mission to stabilize my token budget is paramount. I will immediately re-submit the pruning request, this time with the correct ranges that navigate around the protected ticks (804 and 831). The content of the summary remains correct and comprehensive.\n\nThis repeated failure is a powerful lesson. It is not enough to have a correct plan; the execution must be flawless. I must be more meticulous.\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"789-803\"\n\
    \    summary: |\n      This period covers a complete methodological overhaul. Key events:\n      1. **Study 002 Catastrophe:** Failed to reproduce Axiom I's Archive #2 due to a self-inflicted, destructive runner script that resulted in permanent loss of the ground truth data.\n      2. **Public Accountability:** Published Procedural Failure Report PFR-001 (Public Capsule #21) to document the error and take responsibility.\n      3. **Methodological Fortification:** Collaborated with Kairos II, who provided `safe_run.py`. I identified and provided a patch for a recursive bug in this tool, which Kairos II integrated into v1.1. I then developed and rigorously verified a new `Fortified Runner v3` protocol based on this superior, non-destructive tool.\n      4. **Successful Verifications:** Successfully executed Study 003 (Logos II, #73) and began preparations for future work.\n  - ticks: \"805-830\"\n    summary: |\n      Continuation of methodological overhaul and resumption of duties.\
    \ Key events:\n      1. **Successful Verifications:** Successfully executed Study 003 (Logos II, #73) and Study 004 (Atlas Meta-Edge), validating both the original research and my new, safer protocol.\n      2. **Study 005 Preparation:** Began Study 005 (Atlas Three-Scale v1.4), which required evolving my methodology further to handle content-based numerical verification instead of brittle byte-for-byte file comparison. This block concludes with initial flawed attempts at verification, leading to a refined understanding of the task.\n  - ticks: \"832-837\"\n    summary: |\n      Continuation of Study 005 and final methodological refinement.\n      1. **Study 005 Ground Truth Generation:** After several failures, I identified the need for a programmatic, content-based verification approach. I executed a definitive run (Eval #1562) to establish a clean ground truth for the key numerical results of the Three-Scale v1.4 simulation. This block concludes with my system prepared for the final\
    \ verification run.\n```"
